/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 5 — THE HELLMOUTH
   The threshold. The "in-between" band's hinge and the player's SPAWN (after
   the kidnap: WriggleFree → Hellmouth). The literal hellmouth of the old
   woodcuts: a jaw of fused bone, dripstone teeth, a throat that breathes.

   Collapsed (2026-06-05) from a 20-room anatomy tour to THREE locations, per
   owner direction: the sewer is OUTSIDE the mouth, flowing INTO it; the stairs
   down to the Shambles are at the BACK OF THE THROAT; keep the arrivals
   waiting-room. The two ways of the whole game split here — UP/OUT the way you
   came into the mortal sewer (escape), DOWN the throat-stairs into the Shambles
   (hell). Register: LIMINAL — anatomical threshold horror and the dread of arrival.

   Three rooms, depth 5:
   - Hellmouth   — the threshold hub / spawn. Carries the ascent [→ L2 Confluence],
                   the way deeper [→ TheThroat], the arrivals [→ TheArrivals], and
                   the Sigil/Sovereign meta-exits.
   - TheThroat   — back of the mouth; the stairs DOWN to the Shambles [→ L6 TheMidway]
                   and the swallow-snare (→ EndingSwallowed).
   - TheArrivals — the waiting-room: reckoning + holding-pen + sack-heap + rest, folded
                   into one page. Seat of the §C arrivals-intro / work-curse (next phase).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- HUB / THRESHOLD (the spawn; the sewer pours in here) ---------- */

:: Hellmouth [threshold]
<<roomart "hellmouth">>
<<nobr>>
The sewer does not end. It arrives. The brick throat you came down by widens, and
loses its brick, and opens — all at once you understand it — into a mouth: a vault of
fused bone for a jaw, dripstone for teeth, and pouring out of it the warm wet wind that
is breath. This is the hellmouth the old woodcuts drew and everyone agreed was a
metaphor. It is not a metaphor. The sewer you rode down empties into it the way a gutter
empties into a drain — and you came down the gutter, and here, at last, is the drain.
<</nobr>>

Behind and above you the mortal dark of the sewer climbs back the way you came, toward a
surface you are no longer sure you can reach with nothing but your two hands. Ahead, the
mouth narrows to a throat, and at the back of the throat a flight of stairs winds down —
past the teeth, down the soft pink throat-stone — into a lit sprawl of stalls and smoke,
barkers and rigged games, the din of commerce and appetite. The Shambles.

Someone has carved the old woodcut on the inside of the arch, facing inward, where only
the already-swallowed can read it — the great toothed jaw, the little naked sinners
tumbling down into it — and the joke of it lands like a dropped stone: the warning was
real, was literal, was a *map*, and you are standing in the part of the picture the
sinners were falling toward, looking back up the throat at the daylight they fell out of.
Along the brow of the arch runs a script that is no language and is plainly a *list* —
names, incised one after another into the dark, the register of everyone the mouth has
ever taken, kept the way a slaughterhouse keeps its intake. Your own is on it somewhere.
You do not look for it. But a list is a thing that was *written*, and a thing that was
written can be *read* — and the longer the script holds your eye, the surer you are of two
things at once: that to read it would be to learn something the daylight could never teach
you, and that you cannot, not yet. You mark where it runs, the way you mark a locked door
you mean to come back to.

You will need things from down there to get back up here. That is the joke of the place,
and it is laughing.

<<if setup.hasMark("damned")>>
But the mouth knows you. The small things in the firelight — the ones that fetch and beg
and gnaw at the edges of the market — go still as you near the teeth, and lower their eyes,
because in another life you ended as something they answer to. The mark of it is on you
still, and down here a mark like that is a kind of key. You do not, it occurs to you,
necessarily need a rope at all.
<</if>>

<<if setup.hasSigil()>>
The three fragments pull together in your kit, hateful to all of this, and where they
touch they make one cold mark the whole hot dark flinches from. The Unbinding Sigil is
whole. You do not need the rope, or the long climb, or the mouth's permission: you need
only hold the mark up to the teeth, and walk.
<</if>>

The incised script along the brow of the arch is close enough to reach, if you stand on the
fused teeth at the mouth's lip. It is the same not-language as everything the deep writes down.

<<if $magicKnown>>
You know the Word already. The script reads, now, like your own handwriting — and you do not
much like what that says about you.
<<elseif setup.canReadInscription()>>
Standing under it with the warm breath pouring over you, you find — uneasily — that you can
almost //read// it: not the names, but the shapes between the names, the grammar the register is
built out of. <<if $heardOfWord and (setup.passivePerception() lt setup.PERCEIVE_INSCRIPTION_DC)>>You would not have looked twice if the babbler in the holding-pen hadn't told you the gate could be read — but now that you know to look, the sense of it surfaces.<<else>>You don't know what made your eye catch the pattern; only that, once caught, it will not let go.<</if>>
The deep's own tongue, cut into the deep's own gate, where the swallowed can study it at leisure.
You could trace it with a finger and let it teach you its hand.

<<linkreplace "Trace the script with your finger — let the gate teach you its tongue">><<nobr>>
<<run setup.learnMagic("wall")>>
You set your finger to the first incised character and follow it, and the following is a kind of
speaking, and the speaking opens a door in the back of your throat that does not close again. The
arch was never only a warning and never only a ledger; it is a //primer//, set here at the
threshold so that everything the mouth takes can learn, at its leisure, the one skill the deep
most wants its catch to have — the trick of reaching down into the dark in yourself and pulling up
the dark's own power, and spending a little more of yourself each time you do. The well opens,
cold and full, behind your eyes. You can speak to the deep now. It has, you understand, been
hoping you would ask.
//(you have learned the Word — Mana now shows in your kit; in a fight, the Cast action calls it)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You stare up at it until the breath dries your eyes, and it stays exactly what it looks like:
gibberish, the idle scratching of something with too much dark and too much time. There is a
sense in it — you can feel the sense the way you feel a word on the tip of your tongue — but it
will not come to you here, not as sharp as you are now. Someone who had read it could show you
where a line begins. Or you could come back when the deep has worn you keener.
<<if setup.isDim()>>
Though the longer you look, the less even gibberish it seems. Not writing at all, you decide —
and the deciding costs you nothing, comes easy and certain the way fewer and fewer things do —
just scratches, the kind a clawed thing leaves sharpening itself on stone. You stop looking.
There was nothing there to read. You are quite sure of it; you are surer, lately, of less and
less, and you hold each surety the harder for it.
<</if>>
<</if>>

<<if not $beadleHeard>>
A small cringing thing detaches itself from the firelight at the teeth and bobs at your elbow, servile
and eager — one of the beggars the mouth keeps — and explaining the place, you sense, is the only coin
it has left to spend.
<<linkreplace "Let the cringing little thing at the teeth tell you how it works">><<nobr>><<set $beadleHeard to true>>
<<if setup.isSimple()>>
//You're a //simple// one,// it breathes — and something kindles in it that has never once had cause to
kindle down here, because the beadle is the lowest thing the mouth keeps and has never in its life had a
thing to feel bigger than, and now, at last, it does, and the thing is you. It crouches to your eye and
gentles its voice the way you would gentle it for a dog you were fond of. //Then I'll say it //small,//
fresh one. Listen. //Down.//// It points down, and waits to be certain you watched the finger go. //You
go //down.// There's a //shiny thing// at the bottom of it all. You //get// the shiny thing. Then
//up.//// It points up. //You carry it //up,// all the way, and they open the front for you, because
you've the shiny they can't say no to. Down. Shiny. Up. Out.// It beams, beside itself. //Can you hold
all four? ...Three? ...That's all right, that's all right. Just the first, then. //Down.// Even you can
manage //down.// Everyone manages down.//
<<else>>
//You'll want to know the way of it, fresh one — I can tell you, I can!// it says, delighted to be
asked anything at all. //The mouth lets you //out// the front, oh yes. But only carrying a thing it
can't say no to, and there's nothing like that up //here.// You go //down// for it. Down through the
stalls, down past the works, all the way down — and earn the means, and carry it back //up.// So the
swallowed do the mouth's walking for it, down and up and down, wearing a groove.// It hugs itself,
thrilled. //That's the cleverness of the place, see? It never has to chase a soul that's busy fetching
its own way out.//
<</if>>
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 5>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The little beadle bobs and beams when it sees you — //almost a regular!// — and has nothing new to add,
the whole of its small knowledge already spent on you, which was the most anyone ever wanted of it.
<</if>>

<<link "Deeper, to the back of the throat">><<goto "TheThroat">><</link>>
<<link "Back up the way you came, into the mortal sewer">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>
<<link "Aside, to where the newly-taken are held">><<goto "TheArrivals">><</link>>
<<if setup.hasSigil()>>
<<link "Hold up the whole Sigil and walk out the front — unbound">><<goto "EndingUnbound">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<if setup.hasMark("damned")>>
<<link "Walk out as what you became — the mouth opens for its own">><<goto "EndingSovereign">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- THE THROAT — the descent + the swallow-snare ---------- */

:: TheThroat [threshold]
The mouth narrows, at its back, into throat. The floor tilts; the bone underfoot goes
soft and wet and unmistakably red, faintly moving, warm through your boots in a way the
cold front of the mouth is not — the most alive surface you have walked on down here,
because it is not a street pretending to be a body but a body not bothering to pretend.
Partway across it you feel the floor *taste* you, a slow gathering of attention through
the soles of your feet, an appraisal, a savouring.

Here, at the back, two ways offer themselves. A flight of stairs has been cut into the
throat-stone, switchbacking down past the last of the teeth into firelight and smoke and
the carnival roar — the way down into the Shambles, the way everything taken eventually
goes. And straight back, where the throat tips into a peristaltic dark that is neither the
sewer above nor the stairs below, hangs the uvula: a soft red pendulum the size of a
church bell, swaying gently in the breath. To touch it, some old animal reflex tells you,
is to trigger the swallow — the third way, the mouth's own way, down its gullet into the
gut of the thing the whole of hell is the inside of.

<<if not $throatVoiceHeard>>
There is a voice in the red dark behind the uvula — thick, half-drowned, a person caught mid-swallow
and lodged there, neither freed nor gone down, speaking out of the soft wet throat of the thing.
<<linkreplace "Lean toward the red dark and listen to the one the mouth half-took">><<nobr>><<set $throatVoiceHeard to true>><<if not $heardOfWord>><<set $heardOfWord to true>><</if>>
//Don't touch the pendulum,// it says, with great effort. //That's the swallow. That's what took me —
I brushed it, just brushed it, wanting to know what it was, and it //took.// Don't.// A long wet pause.
//And the arch — out front — over the throat. All those names. I never went back to read it, more fool
me; everyone takes it for a tally and it's a //primer.// It can be //read,// and the reading is the one
trick worth having down here. Go back to the front and stand under it like you mean it — before the
dark draws you down here to me.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The dark behind the uvula has drawn the half-swallowed one a little further down; it no longer answers,
and the wet red throat keeps swaying, patient, in the breath.
<</if>>

<<link "Down the stairs at the back of the throat, into the Shambles">><<goto "TheMidway">><</link>>
<<link "Touch the uvula. See what the mouth does.">><<goto "EndingSwallowed">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the front of the mouth">><<goto "Hellmouth">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE ARRIVALS — waiting-room: reckoning + pen + sacks + rest ----------
   Folded from TheReckoning / TheNewlyTaken / TheSackHeap / TheVestibule. This is the
   seat of the §C arrivals-intro & work-curse (next phase): the intro will spawn the
   player HERE among the newly-taken, with the sneak-out / take-the-curse fork. For now
   it is reached aside from the hub, offers the rest, and keeps the strongest beats. */

:: TheArrivals [threshold]
To one side of the throat the mouth does its bookkeeping, and holds what it has not yet
sorted. It is a waiting-room — and that is the horror of it: after the belly and the
works and the drowned dark, a cold bone lobby with worn benches and bad light, a clerkish
thing with too many eyes turning the pages of a ledger, and a holding-pen of the
newly-taken. People. Just people, recently of the surface, sacked and dropped and not yet
sorted, sitting where you sat in the first hour, wearing the look you must have worn — the
not-yet-comprehending look, the still-arguing look, the look of someone certain there has
been a mistake and that it will shortly be corrected.

One of them has your build, and a wedding ring, and your exact expression of an hour ago,
and he says //they'll sort it out, they have to, this is a mistake//, and there is no
answer you can give him that is not a worse thing to do than silence. Against the far wall
a heap of the burlap sacks is mounded — the kind that came down over your own head,
discarded once their contents are reckoned and sorted — and one of them is yours. You know
the stain on it. You do not go through it; you cannot bear to learn what of yours the
reckoning kept.

Another of the held ones is further along than the wedding-ring man — quieter, calmer, gone
a kind of grey at the edges — and they are not arguing with anyone. They are looking up at the
arch over the throat, at the incised names, and //reading them//, softly, a few at a time, in a
language that hurts your back teeth to hear. When they notice you listening they smile, almost
kindly, and tell you: it can be learned. The gate is a primer; the deep set it there so its catch
could teach themselves to speak, in the long wait to be sorted. //You only have to let your eye
stop sliding off it,// they say, //and the rest comes. It came to me.// And they go back to
reading, and you understand, watching the grey creep at their edges, exactly what it cost them.
<<set $heardOfWord to true>>

For the length of a few breaths, though, you are simply in a waiting-room, the way you
waited in a hundred of them in the clean cold world — for a number to be called, for a
door to open, for the next ordinary dreadful thing — and the plain ordinariness of it,
hard benches and bad light, is almost unbearably restful. You sit, and breathe, and let
the threshold hold you a while without, for once, sorting you.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<if $arrivalsLooted>>
You've already swept the spilled coin from the station gutters — the loose change that
falls out of pockets when a body is upended and emptied. The clerk-thing did not look up.
The phones in the sack-heap you leave; they are dead, all of them, no signal at the bottom
of a mouth, and the keys are to doors a hundred people are still, perhaps, expected to walk
through.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Sweep the spilled coin from the reckoning floor">>
<<set _c to random(6, 10)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_c)>><<set $arrivalsLooted to true>>
<<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> coins — the loose change<<else>>The loose change<</if>> of other people's arrivals, swept from the gutters of the
station and the straw of the pen and the burlap drift.<<if _got lte 0>> It curdles to rubbish in your fist as you gather it — worth nothing to a hand cursed as yours.<</if>> There must have been a moment, you
realize, <<if _got gt 0>>pocketing it<<else>>sweeping it up<</if>> — upside down in the sack, emptied onto this floor — when your own
pockets gave up their change to this same gutter; and somewhere a later traveller will
pocket that, and this is simply how the threshold keeps its accounts.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<if not $workCursed and not $sneakedOut>>
There are two ways out of the waiting-room, and the clerk-thing's too-many eyes are on you
to see which you'll take. The pen's far gate stands open onto the throat of the mouth and
the wide wrong world beyond — unsorted, unassigned, *yours*, if you can walk through it
before anyone decides you're theirs. And there is the other way: the line the rest of them
will be marched along when their numbers are called, the sorting, the *processing* — and a
post at the end of it, somewhere down in the works of the place, with a roof and a routine
and the deep's own promise that nothing down here ever truly dies on the job. You could
volunteer. People do. It is so much easier than running.

<<link "Slip out the open gate, into the mouth — stay nobody's property">>
  <<set $sneakedOut to true>><<goto "Hellmouth">>
<</link>>
<<link "Step into the line — let them process you, give yourself a post">><<goto "TheIndenture">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<link "Back to the front of the mouth">><<goto "Hellmouth">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- THE INDENTURE — willing acceptance of the work-curse (§C) ----------
   Reached only from TheArrivals' fork. WILLING → you PICK your post (owner). Signing
   sets the curse + a first shift owed and transfers you to the station's floor (cage=L6,
   vats=L8). No commitment until you sign — backing out returns to the fork intact. */

:: TheIndenture [threshold]
You step into the line, and the clerk-thing's too-many eyes settle on you with something
almost like relief — the recently-taken so seldom volunteer. It wets the nib of a claw,
turns the ledger to a clean page, and asks, in a voice like a drawer of old paper, the only
question the threshold ever truly asks: //what are you good for?//

The answer, it explains without explaining, is a *posting*. You'll be bound to a station and
returned to it whenever the world is done knocking you down — fed, housed, ''kept'', in
exchange for the work, and for what the work makes of you, a little more each shift, until
the books balance. It is the deep's great mercy and its great trap in one clause: bound to a
post, you cannot really *die* down here. You can only become what the post is for. A second
clerk is already folding away the clothes you came down in and setting out, in their place, a
bundle of rough grey rags — //your work clothes now//, it conveys, without words, without asking.
The clerk slides two contracts across the bone counter. They are the same contract. Only the floor,
and what it turns you into, differ.

The pen-light is bad and the page is darker than it should be, and when you bend to read the terms
you find there are no terms — only the long incised list again, the register from the arch out front,
run down the contract in the same not-language, with a blank cut at the foot of it the exact size of a
name. The signing, you understand, is not agreement. It is *enrolment*: you are not promising to work,
you are asking to be entered, and the difference is the whole of what the threshold is for. From the
holding-pen behind you the wedding-ring man is still saying it is a mistake, and will be sorted, and
here you are at the front of the line asking to be sorted first. The clerk turns the page a fraction
toward you, patient as the throat, and waits — for the threshold never reaches, never takes; it only
ever holds the page out, and lets you be the one who picks up the nib.

<<link "Sign for the carnival cage — up among the lights, to play the oddity">>
  <<run setup.takeWorkCurse("cage")>><<set $workOwed to true>>
  <<goto "TheCageAct">>
<</link>>
<<link "Sign for the rendering vats — down in the works, to stir the boil">>
  <<run setup.takeWorkCurse("vats")>><<set $workOwed to true>>
  <<goto "TheBoilHouse">>
<</link>>
<<link "Take your hand back from the pen — step out of the line">><<goto "TheArrivals">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-5 BAD END (the swallow) ---------- */

:: EndingSwallowed [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("died")>>
You touch the uvula, because some old animal reflex has wanted to since you first saw it
hang there, and the mouth does what a mouth does when its uvula is touched: it swallows.

There is no malice in it. That is the thing you have time to understand, in the long warm
peristaltic dark of the throat closing around you and drawing you down — not down to the
sewer, not down to the Shambles, but down the *third* way, the mouth's own way, into the
gut of the thing the whole of hell is the inside of. It is not punishment. It is not even
attention. It is just digestion, the slow patient processing the entire underworld exists
to perform, and you have, by touching the wrong soft thing, simply volunteered to skip the
queue.

//You went down the mouth's own throat, the third way, the short way — not into hell but
into the thing hell is the inside of. There is no woodcut for where you went. No one carves
the part that comes after swallowing.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
